    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Hypertrophic Cardiomyopathy (HCM) is the most common form of inherited\r\ncardiomyopathy, characterized by the thickening of the ventricular wall in the\r\nheart muscle. The clinical and genetic heterogeneity of HCM, including\r\nsignificant variation in penetrance and presentation as well as extremely low\r\nallele frequencies for most pathogenic variants, complicates the genetic diagnosis\r\nand treatment of this disease.\r\n\r\nTo better understand the phenotypic impacts of genetic variation in various\r\ndiseases, including HCM, one approach is variant-effect mapping in human\r\ninduced pluripotent stem cell (human iPSC) models. This method involves\r\nsaturation mutagenesis by CRISPR-X, where mutations are introduced using a\r\ncatalytically dead Cas9 tethered to a versatile deaminase, enabling precise point\r\nmutations within 50 base pairs of the guide. The resultant cell population exhibits\r\nheterogeneity, with each cell expressing an individual variant within the targeted\r\nloci. Functional assays segregate the variant cell library into pathogenic or benign\r\npools, with subsequent sequencing identifying pathogenic variants. However,\r\nCRISPR-X's efficacy is severely constrained by the requirement for a genomic\r\n'NGG' sequence, known as a Protospacer-Adjacent Motif (PAM), downstream of\r\nthe target (as it limits the number of potential target sites).\r\n\r\nIn this work, I engineered a novel CRISPR-X modality with an ‘NGN’ PAM\r\nrequirement (which quadruples the number of potential CRISPR-X target sites).\r\nDesigning the new modality involved (i) inheriting some Cas9 amino acid\r\nsubstitutions previously shown to generate a less specific PAM preference and (ii)\r\nrational mutagenesis via protein modeling and computational assessment of Cas9\r\nvariants. The first approach to building the new modality, PCR mutagenesis, was\r\nunsuccessful, but protocol optimization experiments were useful in the final step of the accepted engineering process. The second approach, an optimized Infusion cloning protocol with PCR mutagenesis to restore dCas9 functionality, was\r\nultimately successful in building the designed construct. I tested and characterized\r\nthe new CRISPR-X modalities in HEK-293T cells, where I assessed their\r\nperformance relative to wild-type CRISPR-X in a range of PAM settings using\r\nshort-read next-generation sequencing and a custom-built computational analysis\r\npipeline. Upon finding the best of the new CRISPR-X modalities, I found a\r\nhotspot of HCM-associated variants (TNNI3’s Exon 7) via a systematic review of\r\nclinical literature and I conducted saturation mutagenesis of this locus using the\r\nnovel CRISPR-X modality and evaluated its effectiveness as a tool for variant-effect mapping.\r\n\r\nThe newly engineered tool, combined with the strategies uncovered to optimize\r\nsaturation mutagenesis, shows great potential to accelerate the discovery of\r\ngenetic disease-causing variants and enable earlier detection and treatment in\r\nclinical settings.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
